i'm daniel dubrovkin  known as db i am head of engineering at  artsy in new york city  when i started working at artsy we were  building a brand new greenfield project  from  almost from scratch we had a previous  prototype but we decided to delete that  prototype and restart  and we weren't sure what it was going to  be and we wanted to iterate extremely  fast  and extreme programming was great for  the first couple of weeks of that  project because  we would come in the morning do a stand  up figure out where we were yesterday  and just decide what we're going to try  and build today then we'll show it to  the business owner and the business  owner would say we should scrap all that  and go into a completely different  direction  so it's a good case for extreme  programming where you don't need to plan  much except what you're going to do  today the key  for scrum and  other agile methodologies is to give an  opportunity for the business  to pause look around lift everybody's  head  and think about whether we should be  changing direction  or continuing in the same direction as  where we're going at the same time you  don't want to disrupt  engineering every day and hence we do  three week sprints where we all sit in  the same room and talk about what we've  achieved and what we want to achieve  in next three weeks every so often
 

